## Chapter Formalism

It is useful to have some idea from where the theory expressions are derived. The attempt, here, is to keep derivations appropriately concise. The approach follows that of Leader in Ref. [13].

### 1.1 Relation of Final Spin to Initial Spin

A crucial step in any problem is to obtain an expression for the final-state system, which is to be measured, in terms of the initial-state system, which is to be controlled. For scattering problems, one typically (e.g. Ref. [55]) looks for asymptotic wave solutions, e.g.

\[\Psi\sim\left(e^{ikz}+\frac{e^{ikr}}{r}S\right)\chi_{\rm inc}. \tag{2.1}\]

By straightforward methods [55], one can conclude

\[\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}=\left(S\chi_{\rm inc}\right)^{\dagger}S\chi_{\rm inc} =\chi_{\rm inc}^{\dagger}S^{\dagger}S\chi_{\rm inc}, \tag{2.2}\]

or more generally,

\[\frac{d\sigma}{d\Omega}={\rm Tr}\left(S\rho_{\rm inc}S^{\dagger}\right). \tag{2.3}\]

So, to understand any scattering problem, one needs to understand the density matrix, \(\rho\), and the scattering matrix, \(S\).

Following the advice of Jacob and Wick [56] by taking advantage of the helicity quantum number, \(\lambda\), one can consider a pure quantum state,

\[|\psi^{(i)};\vec{p}\rangle=\sum_{\lambda=-s}^{s}c_{\lambda}^{(i)}|\vec{p}; \lambda\rangle, \tag{2.4}\]

with \(c_{\lambda}^{(i)}=\langle\vec{p};\lambda|\psi^{(i)};\vec{p}\rangle\), distributed in an incoherent mixture with probability \(p^{(i)}\)The spin density matrix can be defined [13]

\[\rho = \sum_{i}p^{(i)}|\psi^{(i)};\vec{p}\rangle\langle\psi^{(i)};\vec{p}| \tag{2.5}\] \[\rho_{\lambda\lambda^{\prime}} = \sum_{i}p^{(i)}c_{\lambda}^{(i)}c_{\lambda^{\prime}}^{(i)*}. \tag{2.6}\]

More accurately, this is the spin density matrix in the particle's helicity rest frame. Furthermore, in an inital-state system \(A+B\), the pure state, Eq. 2.4, can be generalized as

\[|\psi^{(i)}\left(A,B\right)\rangle=\sum_{\lambda_{A}=-s_{A}}^{s_{A}}\sum_{ \lambda_{B}=-s_{B}}^{s_{B}}|s_{A},\lambda_{A};s_{B},\lambda_{B}\rangle\langle s _{A},\lambda_{A};s_{B},\lambda_{B}|\psi^{(i)}\left(A,B\right)\rangle. \tag{2.7}\]

The initial spin density matrix (Eq. 2.5) is now generalized

\[\rho_{\rm i}=\sum_{i}p^{(i)}|\psi^{(i)}\left(A,B\right)\rangle\langle\psi^{(i )}\left(A,B\right)|. \tag{2.8}\]

Now, consider the reaction \(A+B\to C+D\), with helicities \(a\), \(b\), \(c\), and \(d\), respectively. If the probability amplitudes to find the final helicities, \(c\) and \(d\), from initial helicities, \(a\) and \(b\), are \(M_{cd;ab}\), the final-state can be expressed in terms of the initial-state (Eq. 2.7):

\[|\psi^{(i)}\left(C,D\right)\rangle=M|\psi^{(i)}\left(A,B\right)\rangle. \tag{2.9}\]

Note, here, \(\psi^{(i)}\) denotes a pure quantum state with probability \(p^{(i)}\). Now, utilizing Eqs. 2.9 and 2.8, the final-state spin density matrix can take the form [13]

\[\rho^{\prime}\left(C,D\right) = \sum_{i}p^{(i)}|\psi^{(i)}\left(C,D\right)\rangle\langle\psi^{(i )}\left(C,D\right)| \tag{2.10}\] \[= \sum_{i}p^{(i)}M|\psi^{(i)}\left(A,B\right)\rangle\langle\psi^{(i )}\left(A,B\right)|M^{\dagger}\] \[= M\left[\sum_{i}p^{(i)}|\psi^{(i)}\left(A,B\right)\rangle \langle\psi^{(i)}\left(A,B\right)|\right]M^{\dagger}\] \[= M\rho_{\rm i}\left(A,B\right)M^{\dagger}\]

