I. Trigger Determination

I am interested in events which are triggered by a \(\pi^{0}\) in the FMS. Thus, it is important to verify that at least one daughter photon from the pion decay could have fired the FMS high-tower trigger. To this end I have developed a procedure to define "trigger photons." A trigger photon must, first, arise from a cluster of towers containing a tower above the trigger threshold. For clusters which yield only one photon, this condition is sufficient to declare the photon a trigger photon. If the photon arises from a two-photon cluster, then, the high tower is associated with the nearer of the two photon centroids. In the rare case when the high tower is equidistant from the photons, the high tower is associated with the higher-energy photon.

